@page "/exsheet/formatting"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Sheet

<DocsPage Product="ExSheet" Title="Formatting"
          Lead="Cell Formats as Excel records them — Number Format, Alignment, Font, Fill and Borders — set from the keyboard, from Format Cells, from the Sheet Toolbar or from your own code, and saved in the Sheet Document.">

    <DocsSection Title="Cell Formats">
        <p>
            A Cell Format has five parts, and each is recorded in the Sheet Document beside the Entries:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Number Format</b>, in Excel's codes: <code>#,##0</code>, <code>#,##0;(#,##0)</code>,
                <code>0.0%</code>, <code>dd-mmm-yy</code>, a colour such as <code>[Red]</code>. Excel's built-in date,
                time and currency formats are shown in the Sheet culture's own form.
            </li>
            <li><b>Alignment</b>: General (numbers right, text left), left, centre or right.</li>
            <li><b>Font</b>: a colour, bold, italic, underline and strikethrough.</li>
            <li><b>Fill</b>: one solid colour.</li>
            <li><b>Borders</b>: a line on each side, in any of Excel's thirteen styles and a colour.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            A format lives at three levels, as in Excel: cell over row over column. A format set on whole columns or
            whole rows is recorded once on them, and covers the cells that hold nothing as well. A fill from whole
            columns or whole rows carries it the same way, recorded once on the columns or rows it fills.
        </p>
        <p>
            <b>A number that does not fit shows <code>####</code>.</b> General rounds its decimals to fit the column,
            as Excel's does; any other format is never shortened, because a formatted figure cut short would read as
            a different figure. Widen the column and the number appears. A number typed into a column you have not
            sized widens it, as Excel's does, and so does setting a Number Format, as Excel's formatting keys do.
            So do Ctrl+5 and Ctrl+U, which set no Number Format, and <code>SetCellFormatAsync</code> when it sets
            underline or strikethrough. Ctrl+B and Ctrl+I widen nothing, nor do a Fill, an alignment, Borders, or
            bold, italic or a Font colour set with <code>SetCellFormatAsync</code>, as Excel's <code>Font.Color</code>
            set from code does not. Format Cells' OK widens when it sets any part of the Font, and the Sheet Toolbar's
            Font Colour widens as Excel's Home tab Font Color button does. An italic number widens its column only to
            the width the same number takes upright, where Excel's Font tab widened it further. A column you sized is
            never widened. A
            column inserted beside a widened one takes its width as one you set, so a longer number typed into it is
            fitted to the column rather than widening it. A number that fits is painted whole, bold too: it
            may use one screen pixel of its cell's right padding, so a column whose edges round narrower on screen
            never cuts it.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(CellFormats)" Mud="typeof(CellFormatsMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/QuarterlyPnl.cs"])">
            Every format here is set in code, with <code>SheetEdit.SetCellFormat</code>, before the document is
            handed to the Sheet.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The Paper and the Ink">
        <p>
            The ground the cells lie on is the <b>Paper</b>, Excel's white, and text in the Automatic colour is the
            <b>Ink</b>, Excel's black — in every colour scheme, as Excel's cells stay white under its dark theme. A
            colour a user recorded reads as it did when it was chosen. What frames the cells — the Headings, the Name
            Box, the Formula Bar, popovers — follows the page's scheme. Switch this site to dark mode to see it. Both
            are Visual Tokens you can set in your stylesheet:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Sheet/paper-and-ink.css" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Borders">
        <p>
            A Border is drawn as Excel draws it: each of Excel's thirteen line styles in Excel's own screen pixels,
            centred on the gridline, so a thick line reaches into both cells, and above the cell's Fill. The lines
            stay on whole screen pixels at every zoom.
        </p>
        <p>
            <b>Lines inside the Selection stay as drawn.</b> The Selection's shade darkens what lies beneath it rather
            than covering it, as Excel's does: a black line stays black, and a Fill darkens. Only the outlines lie
            over the lines: the Selection's at the edge of the range, the Focus's, and a Reference's. This holds in a
            Pinned Column too — Excel's frozen pane — and across its edge. Under ExSheet.MudBlazor the shade is the
            palette's primary mixed into white, and a <code>--ex-selection-fill</code> of your own darkens the cells
            in the same way.
        </p>
        <p>
            Among several ranges, made with Ctrl+click, no range carries the green outline and there is no fill handle,
            and the active cell takes a thin border of one screen pixel inside it, in the outline's green: Excel's active
            cell was observed with that border. Outside the border on its top and left a white line of one screen
            pixel stands between it and the gridline, and on its right and bottom the cell's own ground does, so a
            filled active cell shows its Fill there, as Excel's did. Under ExSheet.MudBlazor the border is the
            palette's primary, as the outline is. <code>--ex-focus-among-ranges-outline</code> colours it, and
            <code>--ex-sheet-selection-ring</code> the white line. While the active cell is edited, the Cell Editor
            covers both, and draws its own outline, <code>--ex-editor-outline</code>, as it does on every Sheet.
        </p>
        <p>
            The <b>Heading Mark</b> shows where the Selection is: each column letter and row number whose column
            or row holds part of it is shaded and coloured, with a line two device pixels thick on its edge toward
            the cells. With the built-in Chrome in the light scheme, the shade is <code>#E1E1E1</code>, or
            <code>#D3F0E0</code> when the whole column or row is selected; the letters and line are
            <code>#217346</code>. The corner's triangle takes that green when every cell is selected. Whole means
            covered by the ranges together, including overlapping ranges. Headings between ranges stay unmarked.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>--ex-heading-mark</code> colours the line, letters and selected triangle;
            <code>--ex-heading-mark-background</code> and <code>--ex-heading-mark-whole-background</code> set the
            two shades. Your Theme's tokens win. ExSheet.MudBlazor uses its palette, with the primary for the line
            and letters. The Headings follow the frame's colour scheme, including their shades in dark mode;
            they do not take the cells' white Paper. Where a Heading covers a side of the Focus or a single range,
            its mark supplies the two device pixels without a second outline beside it. Against the pinned block,
            the outline stays inside the cell.
            The ordinary Focus and single-range outline defaults to two device pixels on every side,
            including at display scales above 100%.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(BordersUnderSelection)" Mud="typeof(BordersUnderSelectionMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/QuarterlyPnl.cs"])">
            Column A is pinned. Select across it, from A3 to D8: the thin lines over the totals and the double line
            under the operating profit stay black under the shade, on both sides of the pinned column's edge.
            Click A1, then Shift+click B2 to see the Heading Mark meet the range. Click a column letter or row
            number to see the whole-axis shade, then the top-left corner to colour its triangle and select every
            cell. Switch between Built-in and MudBlazor, and between light and dark mode.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="From the keyboard, and from your code">
        <p>
            Excel's formatting keys format the Selection, each as one undo step: Ctrl+B, Ctrl+I, Ctrl+U and Ctrl+5
            toggle bold, italic, underline and strikethrough, and Ctrl+2, Ctrl+3 and Ctrl+4 the first three, as in Excel; Ctrl+Shift with <code>~ ! @@ # $ % ^</code> applies
            General, <code>#,##0.00</code>, a time, a date, the currency, a percentage and scientific; Ctrl+Shift+&amp;
            draws an outline and Ctrl+Shift+_ removes the borders. Ctrl+1 opens Format Cells.
        </p>
        <p>
            From code, <code>SetCellFormatAsync(CellFormatChange)</code> sets only the parts the change names, on every
            range of the Selection, as one undo step: a change that sets bold keeps each cell's italic, Fill and Number
            Format. Its borders are relative to each range, so each range gets its own outline.
            <code>CellFormatAt(address)</code> reads back what a cell shows. Like every command, these are refused while
            an edit is open.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FormatFromCode)" Mud="typeof(FormatFromCodeMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/QuarterlyPnl.cs"])">
            Select a range, then press a button. Ctrl+Z undoes each press.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Format Cells">
        <p>
            Format Cells is Excel's dialog: five tabs — Number, Alignment, Font, Border and Fill — in Excel's order,
            opened on the Focus cell's format, with what differs across the Selection shown as Excel shows it. OK sets
            only the parts the user touched, as one undo step; Cancel and Escape set nothing. It opens from Ctrl+1,
            from "Format Cells…" in the Context Menu, and from <code>OpenFormatCellsAsync()</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Over Blazor Server, an IME composition can finish while Format Cells is opening. Its tabs cannot
            accept composed text, so the text and the keys held behind it are dropped under either Chrome.
            No Cell Editor opens behind the dialog; once it has the keyboard, its tabs answer keys as usual.
        </p>
        <p>
            Over one cell that shows a line on exactly one of its edges, the Border tab's Style and Colour open on
            that line, so pressing that edge's button takes the line away. Anywhere else they open on Thin and
            Automatic. Excel's Border tab was seen to open on the line shown over a thick top line, black or
            Automatic, a thin top line and a thick black bottom line, and on Thin and Automatic over a cell with a
            thin top and a thick right, over a range whose cells show different lines, and over a cell with no line.
            The rest is ExSheet's reading, not yet asked of Excel: a line on a left or right edge, any other bottom
            line, a style other than Thick or Thin, a colour other than Automatic or black, and every other
            Selection.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Under the built-in Chrome</b> it is a popover inside the Sheet's box, which scrolls when the box is small.</li>
            <li>
                <b>Under <code>MudSheetChrome</code></b> (<code>ExSheet.MudBlazor</code>) it is a page-level, modal
                <code>MudDialog</code>, with a More Colours button opening MudBlazor's colour picker. More Colours starts in HEX,
                with the current value selected: type a colour with the opening click, and those characters arrive
                there in order, even over Blazor Server. Enter or leaving the field commits the colour.
                Choosing HEX yourself before the opening finishes keeps
                the keyboard there; Escape closes the colour picker before the dialog. It needs a
                <code>&lt;MudDialogProvider /&gt;</code> in your layout, and is refused by name when it first opens
                without one. The keys typed as it closes from Escape, OK or Cancel are the Sheet's, in order, over
                Blazor Server too: ↓ typed straight after Escape moves the Focus, including when the dialog's title or empty space holds the keyboard. OK sets what was touched as it is
                pressed.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            What the dialog offers and what OK sets do not change with the Chrome: the same choices set the same Cell
            Format under either. Switch the Example above to MudBlazor and press "Format Cells…" to compare.
            A Chrome that draws a popup inside its own frame uses <code>FormatCellsContext.HoldKeysForPopup</code>
            before its opening handler awaits, supplying the opener and its key queue; after the hold completes,
            <code>TakeKeyboard</code> hands those keys to the drawn contents. The same callbacks can carry the
            keyboard back to the opener when the inner popup closes, without ending Format Cells.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The Sheet Toolbar">
        <p>
            The Sheet Toolbar is Excel's Home tab, above the Formula Bar. It is off until you set
            <code>ShowToolbar</code>, and the Sheet's <code>ViewportHeight</code> includes it. Each item follows the
            Focus cell — Bold shows pressed over a bold cell — acts on the Selection as one undo step, and is greyed
            out while a cell is edited. A press leaves the keyboard on the Sheet. Over a number that shows
            <code>####</code>, Underline and Strikethrough widen a column you have not sized, as Ctrl+U and Ctrl+5
            do, and so does the Font Colour, by its face or a colour from its list, as Excel's Home tab Font Color
            button does. Bold, Italic and the Fill Colour widen nothing, as Ctrl+B, Ctrl+I and Excel's Fill Color
            button do not. The toggles and the colours set through <code>SetCellFormatFromFocusAsync</code>, which
            widens as Excel's Home tab buttons do; <code>SetCellFormatAsync</code> widens no column for a colour, as
            Excel's <code>Font.Color</code> set from code does not.
            Ctrl+Z typed straight after a colour choice undoes its colour and any widening together,
            even while the list closes over Blazor Server.
        </p>
        <p>
            What it holds, and in which order, is yours to declare in <code>ToolbarContent</code>.
            <code>DefaultToolbarRow</code> is ExSheet's row; a <code>ToolbarRow</code> is a row of your own, and a
            <code>ToolbarButton</code> an action of your own, greyed out while an edit is open like ExSheet's items.
            ExSheet's items are <code>BoldItem</code>, <code>ItalicItem</code>, <code>UnderlineItem</code>,
            <code>StrikethroughItem</code>, <code>FontColourItem</code>, <code>FillItem</code>,
            <code>BordersItem</code>, <code>AlignLeftItem</code>, <code>AlignCenterItem</code>,
            <code>AlignRightItem</code>, <code>NumberFormatItem</code>, <code>PercentItem</code>,
            <code>CommaItem</code>, <code>FormatCellsItem</code> and <code>ToolbarSeparator</code>, so a row of
            your own can hold any of them too.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Bind <code>ShowToolbar</code> both ways</b> and the user can hide and show the toolbar with Ctrl+F1,
                Excel's key for the ribbon. Whether it is shown is yours to keep.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>KeyTips.</b> Release Alt on its own, or press F10, and each row shows its letter: ExSheet's row is H,
                as Excel's Home tab is, and its items carry Excel's letters, so Alt, H, 1 sets bold and Alt, H, O, E
                opens Format Cells, as Excel's Format menu reaches it (read on Windows). A list a KeyTip opens takes
                the keyboard under either Chrome: the arrows move among its choices and Enter or Space makes the one
                they have reached. Escape goes
                back one level, as Excel's did when read on Windows: from a list to its row's letters, then to the
                rows', then to the Sheet. The keys can be typed at once, over Blazor Server too: the letters straight
                after Alt or F10 wait for the KeyTips and reach them ahead of any typed once the toolbar has the
                keyboard, and the keys typed while a list opens or closes, or while the
                keyboard goes back to the Sheet, wait for the keyboard and go where it lands, so Alt, H, 1 typed straight
                off sets bold, and Alt, H, H, ↓, →, Enter fills the cell from the palette, as typed one at a time. The
                Number Format list's More Number Formats… opens Format Cells; Escape typed immediately after
                choosing it closes Format Cells under either Chrome. Your rows and buttons have a KeyTip where
                you set <code>KeyTip</code>; two letters that collide at one level
                are refused when the toolbar renders.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>The look is the Chrome's.</b> The built-in Chrome draws plain buttons with inline-SVG pictures;
                <code>MudSheetChrome</code> draws MudBlazor's controls; under either the toolbar is one Tab stop. A Chrome
                of your own draws each item from its <code>ToolbarItemContext</code>: a list of its own opens when
                <code>OpenRequest</code> changes, and once it holds the keyboard it calls <code>ChoicesOpened(true)</code>
                and answers the keys that returns, typed on the toolbar while the list was on its way; it closes when
                <code>CloseRequest</code> changes; as it begins to close from a key or a choice it calls
                <code>ChoicesClosing</code> with that key and passes on every key typed in it, and once it has gone it
                calls <code>ChoicesClosed</code>, and the toolbar takes the keyboard on from there.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(Toolbar)" Mud="typeof(ToolbarMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/QuarterlyPnl.cs"])" CodeOpen="true">
            Select a few cells and press "Mark reviewed", or press Alt and then R, M. Try Alt, H, O, E to
            open Format Cells, or choose a Font Colour and press Ctrl+Z straight away to undo it. The
            same keys work with either Chrome.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

